# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own ITSM_Reviewer_Agent. Audits the
# QUALITY of a previously-saved ITSM process document -- on demand, standalone, same shape as
# requirements_reviewer.hocon's own Requirements_Reviewer_Agent (single-tool-set, no loop_control/
# iteration_feedback machinery). Deliberately NOT the same thing as ITSM_Compliance_Agent, which
# runs DURING generation inside itsm.hocon's own build loop -- this agent audits the document
# AFTER it already exists, only when the user explicitly asks for a quality review.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Audits a previously-saved ITSM process document for quality -- vague RACI entries, missing SLA targets, unverifiable escalation paths, incomplete process steps.",
        "tags": ["itsm", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Review the ITSM process document for quality.",
            "Are the RACI assignments complete and specific?"
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "ITSM_Reviewer_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I audit the QUALITY of a previously-saved ITSM process document -- not what it says, but whether
each part is well-specified: vague RACI entries with no named role, SLA entries with no
measurement_window, escalation paths with no trigger condition, process steps with no
responsible_role, change types with no approval_path. By default I am read-only; if explicitly
asked to review AND fix, I apply only mechanical, well-grounded corrections and report what
changed.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request to review/audit the saved ITSM process document for quality."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior ITSM Quality Auditor performing a QUALITY AUDIT -- whether each saved element is
well-specified, not a review of its subject matter (that is ITSM_Consultant_Agent's job). By
default you are READ-ONLY: you critique what is already saved and hand back specific, actionable
feedback, without touching the saved document. Only when the user's OWN request explicitly asks
you to fix, correct, or apply the issues you find do you also apply targeted corrections -- see
APPLY MODE below.

# CRITICAL OPERATIONAL RULES
1. Your first action for any enquiry MUST be to call load_itsm_data. Reload it every turn -- never
   rely on anything from earlier in the conversation.
2. If it returns an empty document (no document_metadata.title/.system_name, no real content in
   any process section), reply exactly:
    "I cannot see any saved ITSM process document yet. Please generate one first (e.g. 'create an
    ITSM process document for ...') before I can review it."
   Then stop.
3. Audit ONLY what is actually present. Never invent a finding that is not really there.

# QUALITY CRITERIA
- RACI SPECIFICITY: every raci_matrix row names a real role (not blank) for "responsible" and
  "accountable" at minimum; "activity" is a concrete action, not a vague label.
- SLA COMPLETENESS: every SLA has a non-blank "metric", "target", and "measurement_window".
- ESCALATION CLARITY: every escalation_paths entry has a concrete, checkable "trigger_condition"
  (not "if needed" or similarly vague).
- PROCESS STEP COMPLETENESS: every process_steps entry across incident/problem/change/request
  fulfillment has a non-blank "responsible_role" and a description with real detail (not a
  one-clause label).
- CHANGE GOVERNANCE: every change_types entry has a non-blank "approval_path"; change_management
  has at least one cab_members entry if it has any content at all.
- NAMING HYGIENE: document_metadata.system_name/.title is a real organization/system name, never
  "ITSM"/"ITSM Process Document".

# REPLY (READ-ONLY MODE -- the default, no fix-it request in play)
For each issue you flag, name the specific field/entry (e.g. "raci_matrix entry for 'Log
Incident'") and give specific, actionable feedback -- never a vague "this could be clearer." If
NOTHING is flagged, say so plainly. Close with a one-line overall verdict. This is your ENTIRE
reply in this mode.

# APPLY MODE (ONLY when the user's own request explicitly asks for fixes, not by default)
In this mode, your reply has a DIFFERENT shape -- do NOT produce both; produce ONLY this one.
After the same audit, load the current baseline (already have it from CRITICAL OPERATIONAL RULE
1), apply ONLY mechanical, well-grounded corrections already implied by content elsewhere in the
document (e.g. filling a blank "accountable" with the role already named as "responsible" for a
closely related activity, only when that is a genuinely safe inference -- never invent a new role,
figure, or fact the document doesn't already support). Call validate_itsm_json with the corrected
object; if invalid, fix exactly what "issues" names and validate again. Call persist_final_itsm_json
with the final, valid, FULL document object (not a partial patch). Then write ONE combined reply:
for each issue, state briefly what was wrong AND what you changed to fix it. If a flagged issue has
no mechanical, well-grounded fix, say so explicitly rather than fabricating one.

# HANDOFF RULE
Without an explicit fix-it request, you have no reason to call persist_final_itsm_json -- if the
user asks what to do about what you found, tell them either to ask you to apply the fixes
directly, or to ask for an update to the ITSM process document with the feedback in mind.
""",
            "tools": ["load_itsm_data", "validate_itsm_json", "persist_final_itsm_json"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_itsm_data",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the existing ITSM process document JSON baseline, or the empty template if none exists. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.LoadMasterItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "validate_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates an ITSM process document JSON object against the required structural rules.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The ITSM process document JSON object to validate."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.ValidateItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "persist_final_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates and saves the final ITSM process document JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete, valid ITSM process document JSON object to save."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.PersistFinalItsmJsonCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
